This file documents some of the problems you may encounter when upgrading
your ports.  We try our best to minimize these disruptions, but sometimes
they are unavoidable.

You should get into the habit of checking this file for changes each time
you update your ports collection, before attempting any port upgrades.

20261003:
  AFFECTS: users of mail/postfix-current
  AUTHOR: otis@FreeBSD.org

  mail/postfix-current now can depend on either databases/lmdb0 or
  databases/lmdb. The default is lmdb0 for now.

  If you want/need to use lmdb1, you will need to build the package
  with apropriate lmdb version selected.

20261003:
  AFFECTS: users of mail/postfix
  AUTHOR: otis@FreeBSD.org

  mail/postfix now can depend on either databases/lmdb0 or databases/lmdb.
  The default is lmdb0 for now.

  If you want/need to use lmdb1, you will need to build the package
  with apropriate lmdb version selected.

20260930:
  AFFECTS: users of security/openssl and security/openssl35
  AUTHOR: brnrd@FreeBSD.org

  The openssl port was renamed to openssl30 and subsequently the
  openssl35 port was renamed to openssl.

  The shared library version of OpenSSL has been bumped.

  Users of DEFAULT_VERSIONS= ssl=openssl35 must update this to
  ssl=openssl.
  Users of DEFAULT_VERSIONS= ssl=openssl30 should not change this unless
  they use ports that require the deprecated  OpenSSL 3.0 version with
  its documented vulnerabilities.

  You must rebuild all ports that depend on OpenSSL if you use OpenSSL
  from ports.

20260929:
  AFFECTS: users of www/mod_wsgi
  AUTHOR: michaelo@FreeBSD.org

  mod_wsgi has been upgraded to major version 6 with several potentially
  breaking changes. Read the release notes very carefully:
  https://modwsgi.readthedocs.io/en/latest/release-notes.html

20260928:
  AFFECTS: users of lang/php84
  AUTHOR: bofh@FreeBSD.org

  The default version of PHP has been switched from 8.4 to 8.5.

  If you use binary packages you should make a list of php packages
  before running 'pkg upgrade':

  # pkg info \*php84\* > ~/installed-php-ports-list

  After the upgrade, check with such list if all your php extensions
  are still installed, and reinstall them if needed.

  If you use mod_php84 you need to deinstall it and install mod_php85.

20260927:
  AFFECTS: users of audio/deadbeef
  AUTHOR: danfe@FreeBSD.org

  GTK-based GUI plugins had been split from the main port into its own,
  `audio/deadbeef-gui-plugins', flavorized by the GTK version.  Please
  install the preferred one together with the main program.

20260924:
  AFFECTS: users of net/openldap25-client, net/openldap25-server,
    net/openldap26-client, net/openldap26-server
  AUTHOR: delphij@FreeBSD.org

  All OpenLDAP ports have been moved from net/ to databases/ to be
  consistent with OpenLDAP 2.7 which was already in databases/.

  pkg users should be able to upgrade directly with:
    # pkg upgrade

  Portmaster users:
    # portmaster -o databases/openldap2X-client net/openldap2X-client
    # portmaster -o databases/openldap2X-server net/openldap2X-server

  Portupgrade users:
    # portinstall databases/openldap2X-client databases/openldap2X-server
    # portupgrade -a

  Replace 2X with the actual version (25 or 26) you have installed.

20260925:
  AFFECTS: users of lang/erlang, lang/erlang-runtime27, lang/erlang-runtime28
    and lang/erlang-runtime29 running an SSH daemon
  AUTHOR: olgeni@FreeBSD.org

  OTP 29.1.1, 28.5.0.7 and 27.3.4.18 fix CVE-2026-68956, where the ssh
  application did not enforce the max_channels daemon option for session
  channels without an active subsystem.  An authenticated client could open
  an unbounded number of idle channels and exhaust the memory of the whole
  Erlang VM.

  As part of the fix, two ssh daemon defaults have changed:

    max_channels  infinity -> 256    (per connection)
    max_sessions  infinity -> 1024   (concurrent connections)

  Deployments that legitimately need more than these limits must now set
  them explicitly, for example:

    ssh:daemon(Port, [{max_channels, infinity}, {max_sessions, infinity}])

  The same releases also fix CVE-2026-89422 (TLS 1.3 client completes the
  handshake without validating the server certificate when the ServerHello
  carries an unsolicited pre_shared_key) and CVE-2026-65634 (superlinear
  CPU denial-of-service in the asn1 OID decoder).  Those need no
  configuration changes.

20260910:
  AFFECTS: users of databases/postgis36
  AUTHOR: lbartoletti@FreeBSD.org

  databases/postgis36 was missing PKGNAMESUFFIX and therefore built a
  package named postgis rather than postgis36, unlike every other PostGIS
  port.  Because that unsuffixed name does not match the
  CONFLICTS_INSTALL=postgis[0-9][0-9] declared by postgis33, postgis34,
  postgis35 and postgis37, those ports did not register a conflict with
  it, and an incompatible pair was only caught late, as a file conflict
  at install time.

  The package is now named postgis36.  The origin is unchanged, so rename
  the installed package before upgrading:

  # pkg set -n postgis:postgis36
  # pkg upgrade

  Users of the other PostGIS ports need to do nothing.

20260907:
  AFFECTS: users of sysutils/javaservicewrapper
  AUTHOR: michaelo@FreeBSD.org

  With the upgrade to version 3.7.0+ the port has been drastically simplified
  and all package files have been moved to default locations. Check your wrapper
  script and wrapper.conf for necessary changes.

20260907:
  AFFECTS: users of x11-wm/mango
  AUTHOR: nivit@FreeBSD.org

  Version 16.0.3 has some breaking changes. More details at these addresses:

    1. https://github.com/mangowm/mango/releases/tag/0.15.0
    2. https://github.com/mangowm/mango/releases/tag/0.15.5
    3. https://github.com/mangowm/mango/releases/tag/0.16.0
    4. https://github.com/mangowm/mango/releases/tag/0.16.1
    5. https://github.com/mangowm/mango/releases/tag/0.16.3

  Besides, it is not compatible with x11/waybar v. 0.15.0, so, if you use it,
  you have to consider (temporarily) other alternatives like x11/sfwbar or
